{
  "requirement": "`habeas add-input --item <file>:<line>` MUST parse the file under {cite}`requirements.references.outlines` and add or refresh one\noutline input for every outline node declared on that line. A named node is\ndeclared on the line containing its name; an anonymous node is declared on the\nline where it starts. Each input MUST store the selected `file` under the path\nrules in {cite}`requirements.ci.schema`, the node's selector, and its structural\nhash. The command MUST report every selected node and reject a line on which no\nnode is declared.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "## Verdict\nThe core of `habeas add-input --item <file>:<line>` is implemented and tested well. I am returning false because of two spec deviations in how \"declared on a line\" is computed. Neither is acknowledged by a FIXME, and the tests don't cover them.\n\n## What is implemented correctly\n- **CLI wiring.** `src/main.rs` (`enum[Command]` / `AddInput`, and `main`) uses clap groups to require exactly one target and exactly one mode. `--item` is passed through as `InputMode::ItemsAtLine`.\n- **Parsing and line lookup.** `selectors_for_mode` in `src/add_input.rs` does the following:\n  - It splits `<file>:<line>` with `rsplit_once(':')` and parses the line number.\n  - It parses the file through the outline cache, which uses the language profile or the plain-text profile.\n  - It collects `declared_on_line(line)` and rejects an empty result with the message \"`<file>:<line>: no outline node is declared on this line`\".\n- **File path.** The file is stored relative to the project root when it lies inside it, otherwise as the resolved path. This matches the `requirements.ci.schema` path rules. The external-source test covers allow and disallow.\n- **Stored input and reporting.** `InputSelector::to_input` stores `file`, `item` and `hash`, with no source text. `update_inputs` reports each selected node as added, refreshed or unchanged. Every node declared on the line produces its own input, so both `fn[main]` and `fn[main]/fn[nested]` appear.\n- **Outline pieces.** `plain_text` makes each non-blank line an anonymous `line` node declared on its own line. `Outline::declared_on_line` selects nodes whose `declaration_line` equals the requested line, and `Builder::finish` and `Builder::walk` populate that field.\n- **Tests.**\n  - `add_input_modes_capture_outline_nodes_and_refresh_idempotently` checks:\n    - same-line nested nodes;\n    - a plain-text anonymous `line@…` node;\n    - idempotent re-add;\n    - rejection of a comment line, a blank line, a non-numeric line and a missing file, each leaving the record unchanged;\n    - the exact error text;\n    - hash stability under an edit to an ordinary comment and hash change under a doc-comment edit.\n  - `add_input_requires_one_target_and_mode_and_resets_changed_records` covers refresh in place and rejection when no target or mode is given.\n  - `add_input_external_sources_respect_allow_and_disallow` covers the external-path case.\n  - `src/outline/tests.rs` covers `declared_on_line` for same-line impl and fn.\n\n## Gaps\n1. **Named nodes with custom naming are declared at the node's start, not at their name.** `Builder::name` returns `node.start_byte()` for `NameRule::Custom`. That applies to Rust `impl[...]` and `extern[...]`, Markdown sections and CSS rules.\n   - The requirement says a named node is declared on the line containing its name.\n   - For a Rust impl the name is the self type and trait path. When rustfmt breaks a long impl header (`impl<T: A, U: B>` then `SomeTrait<T> for Foo<U>` on the next line), the implementation declares the node on the `impl` line. The name is actually on the following line.\n   - Looking up the line that contains the name would therefore report \"no node declared\".\n   - No test exercises this.\n2. **Anonymous nodes use the grammar node's start, not the node's start including attached siblings.** `Builder::walk` uses `child.start_byte()` for `declaration_byte` when the node is anonymous. `OutlineNode.line_start` is documented to include attached attributes and doc comments, and `requirements.references.outlines` says attached siblings are part of the node for its line range.\n   - For example, `#[cfg(test)]` on one line followed by `use x;` on the next gives `line_start` on the attribute line but `declaration_line` on the `use` line.\n   - It is arguable which line \"where it starts\" means, but the code and the requirement text are not clearly aligned.\n   - The tests exercise anonymous nodes only for plain-text `line` nodes. No test covers an anonymous Rust node (`use`, item macro) or one with attached attributes.\n3. **Minor test gaps.** There are no `--item` tests for non-Rust, non-plain-text languages (Markdown, TOML, CSS and so on). There is no test for line `0`, and none for a file with a syntax error. These are lower-risk, since they go through the shared outline code.\n\n## Marking\nImplementation marking looks complete. `{impl}` appears on `add_input`, `selectors_for_mode`, `update_inputs`, `to_input`, `resolve`, `Command`, `main`, `plain_text`, `name`, `walk`, `finish` and `declared_on_line`. The other `update_inputs` behaviors (captured definitions, resetting the determination to false) are covered by `add_input` and the related requirements it also implements.\n\nBecause gaps 1 and 2 are real deviations or ambiguities from the stated \"declared on\" rule, and are neither tested nor acknowledged, the judgment is false.",
